WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.996
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.390
BUZZ HAYS: We're going to be
talking about revolutionizing

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.120
media workflows through what we
refer to as intelligent content

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:13.620
discovery.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.912
So we'll walk you through this.

00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:17.520
And we have some demonstrations
and other exciting things,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.110
but first a little
housekeeping here.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.670
We have three speakers today.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.620
We have Bryan Rutkowski, who
is a Customer Engineer in Media

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.967
for Google Cloud.

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:28.800
We have Drew Hodon,
who's a Machine Learning

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.270
Specialist with Google Cloud.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.729
My name is Buzz Hayes,
I'm the Global Lead

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:36.180
of Media Entertainment Solutions
for the Google Cloud Solutions

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.360
Architecture Team.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.775
Quickly, our agenda for today--

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.650
we're going to start
with an overview of what

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.200
this notion of intelligent
content is all about.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.190
We'll give you a walkthrough,
some of the steps to how

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.330
that solution is put together.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.240
We'll talk about
how this applies

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.500
to archives and online or
cold storage in the cloud.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.820
We'll also be talking quite a
bit about the use of AI and ML

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.800
tools for media.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.530
You'll have a demo of
one of our partner tools

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.790
that surfaces in a very
elegant visual fashion,

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.750
will surface machine
learning data, metadata,

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.250
that makes it very easy
to search your content.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.890
We'll also be doing a
discussion about how Discovery--

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.520
our pals at Discovery,
thank you for being here--

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.540
are using intelligent content
in their monstrous archive--

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.543
and awesome archive,
I should say.

00:01:32.543 --> 00:01:33.960
And we'll also
tell you how to get

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.280
started on this whole process.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.998
For those of you who
have the Next App,

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:40.540
if you go to this
particular session,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.670
you'll find a link for Dory.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.110
So if you have any questions,
feel free to enter it there.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:45.660
We also have microphones here.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.270
So feel free at the end-- we'll
have some time for Q &amp; A--

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.670
to step up to the mic
and ask the questions.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.910
So without further
ado, let's take

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:57.820
a look at what intelligent
content is all about.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:02.560
So we're going to take a top
view here of the process.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.195
It starts with
consolidating the media.

00:02:04.195 --> 00:02:05.820
You've got media in
all sorts of places

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.340
and in all sorts of
forms, so you really

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.620
want to bring that all
into a single platform,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.410
or at least a similar platform.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:15.960
It may be cloud, but it may
be multi-cloud, in this case.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.050
But you want to put all
your assets together,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.390
then you want to apply
machine learning tools.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:22.890
And we have a lot
of automated tools

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.840
now that are not
only easy to use,

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.880
but they're also easy to
modify, which is great.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.020
And Drew will get into some
of that in a little bit.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.710
But this allows you
to gain new insights

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.620
and apply metadata to both
your audio and visual data.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.890
That includes documents
and photographs, as well.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.950
And then once your
content has intelligence,

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.848
basically, you have the
power of your entire archive

00:02:46.848 --> 00:02:47.640
at your fingertips.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.830
A lot of archives
right now don't

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:54.330
exist in a meaningfully
searchable form.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.820
And we'll get into why
that is in a minute.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.070
But ultimately, you want to
be able to find everything

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.362
that's in there and leverage
that for whatever purpose.

00:03:01.362 --> 00:03:03.210
So the vast majority
of media archives,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.700
especially at the Hollywood
studios and television

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.270
entities, exist in a form
that can't even be viewed,

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.780
let alone monetized.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:14.010
So we have some solutions to
help figure that out, as well.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.830
And then once the library is
made meaningfully searchable

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:21.420
by adding the metadata using the
machine learning and AI tools,

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.180
you can begin to broaden
your use of these tools

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.790
to gain further
insights and derive

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.470
analytics from that content,
which can be very important.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:31.770
And so it increases
the level of insight

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:35.640
you have into this content
pretty dramatically.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.390
So let's go a little
bit deeper into this.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.130
Here's how it works.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:42.510
You're basically starting by
adjusting the content into GCS,

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.830
into cloud storage.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.870
And the content can come
from a variety places,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:50.880
whether it's already
on some spinning disk

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.840
someplace on a server, some
physical storage, like tape,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.365
for example, a data tape.

00:03:56.365 --> 00:03:57.490
It could also be videotape.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.010
It could be film, other things.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.720
But they have to be
digitized, obviously.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.120
So once all of those
assets are available,

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.030
you ingest those, import
those into Cloud Storage.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.990
And then using a variety
of the GCP tools,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:14.970
we can then apply all of
the various relevant tools

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.040
to derive these insights.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:18.248
So whether you're trying to
derive visual information

00:04:18.248 --> 00:04:22.380
from images, you're trying
to derive speech from audio,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.390
you're trying to derive
text from images,

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.400
a variety of things
can be leveraged,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.990
and mostly automatically,
which is great.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:32.065
And then you can start
to visualize that data

00:04:32.065 --> 00:04:32.940
through various ways.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.940
And you'll have a demo,
again, in a few minutes,

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.810
of one of the
partner solutions we

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.910
have for visualizing that data.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.180
How many of you use media
asset management software

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.870
at this point?

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:44.430
Yeah?

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.442
How many do you absolutely
love the tool you have?

00:04:47.442 --> 00:04:48.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:48.960
Wait.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.010
I don't see any hands.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.750
Something's wrong.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.510
OK, it was once asked
to me by a studio client

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:57.000
why media asset
management can't be

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.260
as simple as a Google search.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.870
And the beauty of the
Intelligent Content Discovery

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.717
is that it can be as
simple as a Google search.

00:05:03.717 --> 00:05:05.550
You want to be able to
ask questions the way

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.208
the humans who are looking for
the answers are asking them--

00:05:08.208 --> 00:05:09.750
that scene with the
woman in the blue

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.125
dress, and she
said good morning.

00:05:11.125 --> 00:05:11.780
She had a hat.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.030
You know, that kind of thing--

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.690
you want to be able to surface
meaningful data from even

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:16.753
obtuse queries like that.

00:05:16.753 --> 00:05:19.170
And then once you have that,
you can activate this content

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.462
in a variety of different ways.

00:05:20.462 --> 00:05:23.250
And I think there are many gold
mines sitting out there that

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.080
are yet to be explored,
just because they

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.710
don't have meaningful
metadata attached to them.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.030
So there are a number of
different formats out there.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:37.350
And apologies to any of you who
are die-hard supporters of any

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.890
of these formats I'm about
to dis here a little bit.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:44.110
But it's difficult to find
insights in your content

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.200
if it's sitting on some
physical media that

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.950
hasn't been brought into
a platform like Cloud

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.880
to be able to analyze it.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.880
So you're still talking
about a lot of LTO tape,

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.950
or some form of data tape.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.950
And as many of you
know, a tape in it

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.010
itself is not
inherently archival.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.740
I guess it depends on your
definition of archival.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.195
If you talk to
LTO manufacturers,

00:06:06.195 --> 00:06:07.820
they'll probably tell
you that the tape

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:11.550
will last 35 years, which
is true if you don't use it.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.050
And then if you
start to use it, you

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.490
start getting wear and tear.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.170
That shelf life ends up
getting much, much smaller.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:22.380
So you really rely on
the physical storage.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:23.870
And luckily, we
have great partners

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:26.370
like Iron Mountain who do a
very good job of climate control

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.110
and things for
physical asset media.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.642
But it really requires
diligence to make sure

00:06:30.642 --> 00:06:31.850
that that data is still good.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:33.390
And you have to check it.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:36.060
Also with LTO, you tend
to have to migrate it

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.720
to whatever the newest format
is, because most of these LTO

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.070
formats are not backward
compatible more than maybe

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:42.270
a version.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:47.000
So you end up with significant
physical damage possibilities--

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.823
bit rot, for example, if it
hasn't been checked in a while.

00:06:49.823 --> 00:06:51.240
So there's a number
of reasons why

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.140
LTO is becoming a
bit more problematic,

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:56.190
especially given the volume
of content that we have now.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.480
It's almost too much.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.630
Major studios will migrate their
entire collections to new LTO

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:01.810
formats every few years.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.340
And it's becoming prohibitively
expensive to do that.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.950
The manual tagging of assets--
you know, a lot of people

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:09.120
have been applying metadata.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.820
The term's been around forever.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.540
And they've been
using human resources

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:13.990
to do that, which is great.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:16.443
It's a bit error prone
because, if you have 50 humans,

00:07:16.443 --> 00:07:18.610
they're going to be
interpreting things differently.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.985
They're not all going to give
the same results on things.

00:07:20.985 --> 00:07:22.590
Plus, it's a little
bit error prone.

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.300
And you're basically
having to watch

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.850
things, transcribe things.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:30.220
And really, it requires a
lot of paying attention.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:31.920
And there's so much
content that it's

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.570
almost impossible
for human beings

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:34.830
to do all this transcription.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.500
So that's another reason
why using something

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.340
like Intelligent Content
Discovery makes a lot of sense.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.450
You also find in
this day and age,

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:45.955
if you're working
especially in feature films,

00:07:45.955 --> 00:07:47.580
you have day and date
releases of films

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.910
that have to go out in multiple
formats to dozens of countries.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.680
I was talking with an
executive at Disney.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.390
And one of the latest
Marvel releases

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:57.745
went out in over 100
versions on the same day.

00:07:57.745 --> 00:08:00.120
So there's a lot of content
that has to be taken care of,

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:06.180
and some of that content has to
be filtered because there are

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.598
certain regional requirements.

00:08:08.598 --> 00:08:10.140
You know, there are
censorship boards

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.690
in various parts of the country
that have very strict rules.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.548
There's often inappropriate
content for various audiences.

00:08:15.548 --> 00:08:17.340
There's other things
that has to be trapped

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:18.382
before it goes out there.

00:08:18.382 --> 00:08:20.380
So compliance is a big issue.

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.910
And so using ML tools
to automatically derive

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:25.410
that those insights can be very
helpful, especially when you

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.920
have these massive
volumes of content.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.200
And again, to find
that traditionally,

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.059
the inappropriate
content, required a person

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:35.700
having to sit there and find it.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.830
So what if you knew
what your content knows?

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:42.450
So the idea is if
you're consistently

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.930
applying the same tools
and the same metadata,

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.820
you're surfacing
the same metadata

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.580
for your entire
library, you're going

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.930
to find consistency is
actually of great benefit.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.928
You'll see in this
demo in a few minutes

00:08:53.928 --> 00:08:56.220
that you can now surface
things that are like something

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:56.850
you found.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.710
So if you found something
you really like,

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:00.270
by having consistent
metadata, you

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:01.833
can also surface
other things just

00:09:01.833 --> 00:09:03.750
like that, which can be
very, very useful when

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:07.660
you're trying to leverage the
assets in your media library.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:11.730
So it makes the entire library,
the life of that library,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:15.990
considerably longer if you find
that you can use these insights

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:18.720
to leverage licensing.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.520
A lot of studios now are finding
that they have great revenue

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:25.650
in stock footage--

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.150
footage that's not
used in the film,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.350
but they shot it for the film.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.600
It's not unusual for film
to have a very high shooting

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.610
ratio these days because
of digital technology.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.340
It used to be when we shot film,
we'd shoot a 10 to 1 ratio.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.020
For every minute of
screen time, there'd

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:38.790
be 10 minutes of capture.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.725
Nowadays it's, not unusual
to have 200 or 300 to 1.

00:09:41.725 --> 00:09:44.350
So there's a lot of content that
never makes it into the movie.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:45.910
So why not monetize that?

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:47.880
But you have to know
where it is first.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.730
So obviously, we want
to be able to reduce

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:51.720
the time it takes to do this.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.970
You need to prepare the
content for your distribution.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:55.345
So we can apply
these tools again

00:09:55.345 --> 00:09:57.920
to find sensitive
content, to derive

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.950
closed captioning, for
example, a number of things.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:04.670
We want optimize the content
and adhere to whatever

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:05.860
compliance process there is.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:08.430
Every country has different
restrictions on that.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.450
And again, we want to create
rich and consistent metadata.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:12.690
That's the key.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:16.040
So as the value that comes
with intelligent media

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.800
content is to really
understand how

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:20.690
to make meaningfully
searchable metadata.

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:23.845
It's easy to surface metadata
that's not very useful.

00:10:23.845 --> 00:10:25.470
We want to make sure
that it is useful.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:28.880
And we provide labels as
part of that to identify

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:30.180
certain objects in the frame.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:32.347
And then we can then search
our content very quickly

00:10:32.347 --> 00:10:33.793
and cost effectively.

00:10:33.793 --> 00:10:35.960
We can also provide
intelligence search capabilities

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:38.720
for media archives and enable
consumers to find their content

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:39.730
very quickly and easily.

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.650
And that's important,
especially in this day and age

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:44.510
with deadlines
being what they are.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:46.570
You also reduce fines
and risks, of which

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:50.360
they are very real when it
comes to inappropriate content.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.450
And you can find new ways
to monetize this content

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:55.520
through the information
that's derived.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:57.800
So let's take a quick
look here at the steps

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.050
on our way to the solution.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:02.900
As you can see
here, basically, you

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.060
identify the right partner
to get the data out

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.650
of your data or your
physical library.

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:10.130
And in many cases, some of
the tools that we have you

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:13.280
can do that yourself, or you can
partner with one of our partner

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:14.540
companies to do that.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:16.190
You ingest your
archives into GCP.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.700
We have partners that
specialize in that, as well.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:21.200
We have partners that help you
migrate your tape libraries--

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.242
your data tape libraries.

00:11:22.242 --> 00:11:23.783
We have other partners
that will help

00:11:23.783 --> 00:11:26.420
your take your physical assets,
like videotape and film,

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:28.670
and migrate those
into the platform.

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:32.690
And then you can generate the
insights from our Google Cloud

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:34.910
ML tools in a variety
of ways, which

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:36.560
we'll talk about a little bit.

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:39.018
And then once you've got that,
you can organize it and make

00:11:39.018 --> 00:11:41.690
it all accessible, which is
really quite simple, again,

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:44.120
because of the consistency
aspect through the metadata.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.060
And then you really get
some meaningful insights

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:48.060
into these tools.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:49.490
And that's the important
thing about most archives,

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.620
because we haven't had the
luxury of that in a while.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:53.703
So here's some of the media
partners that we have.

00:11:53.703 --> 00:11:55.620
And this is by no means
an exhaustive list--

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.160
but from connectivity
to data migration,

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.730
to the ML and AI tools, to
actual content management.

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:03.740
And the list is
growing every day.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:06.320
And all of these
are vital components

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.020
to the ecosystem of the
Intelligent Content Discovery

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:09.960
process.

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:13.070
So very quickly, by moving your
media assets into the cloud,

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:15.290
you'll have unprecedented
control over that content.

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:19.130
However, there's many different
types of media archives.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.402
And most of them
are not digital.

00:12:21.402 --> 00:12:23.360
According to the Academy
of Motion Picture Arts

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:28.130
and Sciences, half of the films
made prior to 1950 no longer

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:29.660
exist in any form.

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:33.810
And that's just because
of neglect and the fact

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:35.560
that it wasn't really
considered something

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:37.790
that people would need to hold
onto until it was too late.

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:38.998
So we're trying to curb that.

00:12:38.998 --> 00:12:40.410
And videotape is even worse.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:42.290
And I think we're about to
realize that in a few years

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:44.290
when we start to go back
to these older archives

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:47.040
and realize we can't
read the data anymore.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.572
So this analog content is
really important to us.

00:12:50.572 --> 00:12:52.280
So we've been working
with media archives

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:54.080
all over the world
to help come up

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:56.360
with solutions to get all
of the content digitized

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.850
and make it intelligently
searchable, so that then it

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:02.060
can be brought to market,
if that's the intention,

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.790
or preserved, which is another
intention of a lot of older

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:08.840
material, but at least be
able to make this content more

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:10.280
stable and searchable.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:13.190
And then by applying
media search,

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:15.328
we can do really cool
things, like just entering

00:13:15.328 --> 00:13:17.120
a line of dialogue, or
the name of an actor

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:18.890
and actress, or even
their character name,

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.390
or what they're
wearing in a scene,

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:22.670
or the location where
they are, and be

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:27.560
able to surface very quickly
the visual content that relates

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:30.680
to those searches, that
are asked in human terms,

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.815
not in Boolean search
terms, for example.

00:13:33.815 --> 00:13:35.690
So if you're using the
power of Google Cloud,

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:37.790
you can definitely find
what you and your audience

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:40.020
is looking for by
applying this data.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:44.540
So with this huge variety
of both digital and physical

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:46.370
assets, we need more
and more efficient

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.665
solutions to be able to
migrate this to Cloud.

00:13:48.665 --> 00:13:51.290
And that's sort of key in all of
this is getting stuff in there

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:52.790
quickly.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:56.330
Here's quickly some overviews
of storage solutions.

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:58.728
Of course, we have a variety
of different use cases here.

00:13:58.728 --> 00:14:00.020
And so we have streaming video.

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:02.450
We have video transcoding,
backup, archival disaster

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.723
recovery, to mention just a few.

00:14:04.723 --> 00:14:06.890
And we have different storage
classes for all those,

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:08.098
as you're probably all aware.

00:14:08.098 --> 00:14:11.330
We also yesterday announced
a new cold line storage

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:15.020
tier, which is arguably
less expensive than tape,

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.970
which is great.

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:19.640
This is for data that's
accessed far less

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:24.500
than what you would normally
use for regional storage.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:26.165
So it's important
to note that you

00:14:26.165 --> 00:14:28.790
have different levels of storage
for all of the different media

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:30.720
needs you have.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:33.980
So it's important
through all of this

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:36.710
that when you're trying
to derive the insights

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:40.730
from this metadata, that it's
put into a useful form where

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:43.250
you can actually read
it and make sense of it.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:44.998
And I think it's
really important

00:14:44.998 --> 00:14:46.790
to know that there are
tools out there that

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:48.440
currently do this for you.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:51.630
I don't know if any of you have
seen the output of just an ML

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.700
process, but
typically, it's just

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:55.740
a gigantic JSON file
with a ton of text

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.660
and numbers and brackets in it.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.490
And it's not the most
friendly thing to work with.

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:04.380
So to show a little bit more
about how these insights can

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.960
be leveraged, I want to
introduce Drew Hodun, who's

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.550
going to walk you
through the machine

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:11.650
learning and AI aspects of the
intelligent content discovery.

00:15:11.650 --> 00:15:12.892
Drew?

00:15:12.892 --> 00:15:13.392
DREW HODUN.

00:15:13.392 --> 00:15:13.892
Awesome.

00:15:13.892 --> 00:15:15.670
Thank you, Buzz.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:18.640
So let's talk a little bit
more about the capabilities

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:21.680
we can implement on our content
once it's in the Cloud--

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:25.750
once we have it in a more
usable, accessible format.

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:27.820
So within Google Cloud,
we provided a variety

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:29.890
of different ways to
use machine learning

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:32.580
from off-the-shelf APIs,
all the way to the latest

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:35.080
and greatest in machine learning
researcher and data science

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:36.010
tools.

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:38.470
We're going to focus mainly
on the off-the-shelf APIs--

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:42.280
the models that Google builds
and maintains using Google

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:46.270
sized data, sets and
Google expertise.

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:48.280
In particular, for
the media archives

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:51.790
that we're talking about, our
Vision API and our Video API

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:54.420
are critical first steps
to tagging your data.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.170
We'll talk about a lot
of different things

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:58.390
that you can get
out of these APIs.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.790
It's worth noting that
you can call these APIs

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:02.500
with a single RESTful call.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:04.870
You don't need to have
a tremendous amount

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:07.630
of compute in Google Cloud
or a tremendous amount of ML

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:08.290
expertise.

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:12.230
It's very easy to add these
into existing applications.

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:13.530
So these APIs will--

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:15.072
I'll talk about some
of the methods--

00:16:15.072 --> 00:16:17.740
but detect a lot of predefined
labels in the models

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:18.610
that we build.

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:21.140
But you'll notice up here,
we have AutoML Vision, AutoML

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:23.657
Video, which we just announced.

00:16:23.657 --> 00:16:25.240
These are really
powerful capabilities

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:26.860
that allow you to
define what you're

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.510
looking for in your content.

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:30.340
You need to provide
some training data,

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:33.110
and we'll tell we'll show you
a really easy way to do that.

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:36.370
But then you can run these
AutoML APIs on your data

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:37.960
and get the tags
and the information

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.850
that you care
about, not the tags

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:42.085
that that's part of just
the Google taxonomy, right?

00:16:42.085 --> 00:16:43.960
That's something we hear
a lot from customers

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:46.997
is the off-the-shelf APIs
that they see across Google

00:16:46.997 --> 00:16:48.580
and other providers
are nice, but they

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:51.460
have really specific things
are looking to detect.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:54.760
And they have on the
ground expertise--

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:56.800
producers, et cetera,
who are looking

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.670
for very specific things, but
aren't ML experts or maybe even

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:01.820
developers.

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:05.510
And so we'll show how
we can integrate that.

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.250
Here are a lot of
different things

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:08.390
you can do with the APIs.

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.710
It's not just limited to
vision or video, right?

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:13.369
There's all the
standard metadata

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:17.000
tagging we get with our
video and vision APIs.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:19.490
We're doing things like logo
detection, landmark detection.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:21.560
We'll actually tell you
latitude and longitude

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.390
if we recognize a landmark.

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:26.960
We'll recognize
faces and sentiments.

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:30.640
And this is sort of the backbone
of your Smart Media Archive,

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:34.010
is just all the tags you
get-- what is in the frame,

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:35.480
on the screen.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:37.040
We can also use speech to text.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:38.850
So this is transcription.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:42.320
If you have an audio file, or
there's audio in your video,

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.570
we'll output in a
highly accurate manner

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:45.688
what is being said.

00:17:45.688 --> 00:17:47.480
If there's multiple
speakers, not an issue.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:49.990
We can handle that, as well.

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:51.920
We do translation.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:56.200
So this is part of that
localization challenge, right?

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:59.770
How do we create
translated transcriptions

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:00.590
of what's going on?

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:02.560
How do we make our
content searchable

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:03.850
in multiple languages?

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:06.137
Even if we're not doing
closed captioning using this,

00:18:06.137 --> 00:18:07.720
it would be great
to know what's being

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:09.670
said in a video
across many languages

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:13.310
to then make it searchable
and discoverable by our users.

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:17.920
And if you have a relatively
small data set of labeled data

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:20.110
and you want to
improve the API, you

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:22.330
can do that using
AutoML Translate.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:26.230
You would provide us
your loadable data,

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:28.540
either a dictionary of
word to word, or a sentence

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:29.512
to sentence.

00:18:29.512 --> 00:18:30.970
And with a click
of a button, we'll

00:18:30.970 --> 00:18:34.180
train an even more
accurate Translation API

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:35.920
just for your
usage, because it's

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.840
using your data to improve it.

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:41.840
We can do sentiment extraction
using the Natural Language API.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:45.410
So is this a generally positive
clip or a generally negative

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:47.600
clip, among other things?

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:51.140
We can classify very
quickly what is in the clip,

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:52.413
like I said.

00:18:52.413 --> 00:18:54.830
One of the things that Video
Intelligence API will also do

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:56.370
is shot detection.

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:58.280
So it helps you to
break up your video

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:01.610
and then discover what is
in these different shots

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.980
so you can either
quickly find and create

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:06.530
content shot for shot,
or in some cases,

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:08.690
I've worked with customers
to actually generate

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.850
content on-the-fly,
using techniques

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:15.075
like the shot detection.

00:19:15.075 --> 00:19:16.450
So let's dive a
little bit deeper

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:20.572
into the Video Intelligence
API and the AutoML Video API

00:19:20.572 --> 00:19:22.030
and talk about the
methods we have.

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:24.370
So with a single
API, you can tell

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:27.918
us to run one or all of
these ML passes on your data.

00:19:27.918 --> 00:19:29.210
So there's the label detection.

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:30.340
And that's, what are we seeing?

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:32.007
What are the actions
that are happening?

00:19:32.007 --> 00:19:33.620
What's on screen?

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:35.757
We'll do text detection or OCR.

00:19:35.757 --> 00:19:37.840
So if there's any text on
screen, you'll get that.

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.510
And that's really valuable
from a search standpoint.

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:42.760
Logo detection-- really
important if you're

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:44.650
looking for content
to build a partnership

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:49.260
or understand how often
a partner is on screen.

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:50.510
We'll do speech transcription.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:54.400
So even though you're
sending the video,

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:57.590
so it's audio and video,
we'll do the transcription

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:59.630
as part of that
single API call, which

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:02.180
makes that a lot easier
for you to implement.

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:03.860
Explicit content
detection is critical--

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:07.010
so understanding
what is on screen,

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:09.410
when it might be more
sensitive, in particular,

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:10.850
for localization.

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:13.280
Different markets care
about different aspects

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:15.530
of explicit content
detection, whether it's

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:18.010
violence, adult
content, et cetera.

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:20.120
And we mentioned the
shot change detection.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.577
All of this can be done
with a single API call.

00:20:22.577 --> 00:20:24.910
Huge amount of information
you get back on your content.

00:20:27.653 --> 00:20:30.070
So some new announcements with
the Video Intelligence API,

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:31.210
in particular.

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:33.400
We used to just offer
batch mode, which

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:37.030
was you had a video file
on Google Cloud Storage,

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:40.310
you would call the API and
point the API at that file.

00:20:40.310 --> 00:20:41.890
We now support file streaming.

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:45.560
So let's say you
have a large file.

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:47.560
You can create a GRPC
streaming connection

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.770
and send that, basically
byte for byte, up to the API.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:52.940
You don't need to
land that in GCS.

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:54.940
Obviously, a lot of
workflows-- and the workflow

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:57.670
we're talking about
recommends that you

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:58.810
have the content on GCS.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:03.070
But let's say you have either
a real time situation or a use

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:05.488
case where the
file is elsewhere,

00:21:05.488 --> 00:21:07.030
and you don't want
to move it, right?

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:07.930
We support that now.

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:09.910
And I mentioned real time.

00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:11.380
We now support live streaming.

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:14.590
So we support HLS,
RTSP, and RTMP.

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:16.900
Great for understanding
live content--

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:19.120
what's being said,
what's on screen.

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:22.210
Super helpful if you're
doing custom ad placement--

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:24.200
just-in-time ad placement.

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:26.650
In the prefetch, for
example, on online content,

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:28.630
you can know in real
time what's on screen

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:32.060
and do quite a lot with that.

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:34.100
So we're going to
show you a demo using

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:39.320
one of our several partners on
the ML Video Intelligence end.

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:43.080
We're going to be using Zuora
Video Intelligence platform--

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:43.730
ZVI.

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:46.640
So ZVI, as you'll
see in a, second it's

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:48.950
a really intuitive
front end GUI.

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:50.660
It's the back end
document model.

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:54.960
And it also applies some custom
ML and clustering ML, as well.

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:58.430
It's a really easy way to use
all these APIs in an accessible

00:21:58.430 --> 00:21:59.420
manner.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:00.800
No coding required.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.590
And you'll see just how powerful
the combination of these two

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:05.830
technologies are.

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:08.230
So behind the scenes how the
solution is going to work--

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:11.770
first, you'd import the
assets into Google Cloud, then

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:12.550
index them.

